  "explanation": {
    "short": "Some Thai words start with two consonants said together: ครู (kru), กลับ (klap), พรุ่งนี้ (phrung-nee). The first letter decides the tone class: the ค of ครับ is low-class, so a short dead syllable gets the high tone.",
    "more": "The pairs you will meet most are กร กล ปร ปล พร คร ขว. Only the first consonant counts for the tone, so กลับ (mid class, dead) is low, while ครับ (low class, short, dead) is high. Remember the marks too: ใกล้ has ้ and falls, while ไกล has no mark and is mid. It is easy to write them wrong, so this pair is a good one to say aloud. In ordinary speech the ร in these pairs is often dropped: ครับ is said \"khráp\" or even \"kháp\"."
  },
